# Snapping a cut onto a keyframe, which is what decides whether an export copies or re-encodes.
#
# ExportPlanner has always known: FindLosslessBlocker returns CutsMissKeyframes, and
# SnappedBoundaries has always computed where the marks would land if moved. Neither was
# reachable — the user learned "a cut resumes mid-GOP" from the status line partway through an
# export that was already running, which is too late to do anything about it.
#
# The out point is the only boundary that matters. A ripple delete of [in, out) leaves segments
# starting at source frame 0 and at `out`, and frame 0 is always a keyframe.

sample clip.mp4 10
open clip.mp4
assert-has-media

# The sample is written with -g 30, so keyframes land every 30 frames.
goto 40
key I
goto 100
key O
assert-marks 40 100

# 100 is not a multiple of 30; the next keyframe at or after it is 120.
key Shift+K
assert-marks 40 120
assert-status keyframe

# Forward, never backward: snapping back would keep footage the user asked to remove.
key Shift+K
assert-status already on a keyframe
assert-marks 40 120

key X
assert-status exports by copying

# ---- and the case it is for -------------------------------------------------------------
reset
open clip.mp4
goto 40
key I
goto 101
key O
key X
assert-status re-encode

# ---- the marks are drawn where they can be aimed at ---------------------------------------
# Keyframe marks appear in the ruler only when the window is short enough for them to be
# individually meaningful; at zoom 1 a long recording would paint the lane solid.
reset
open clip.mp4
goto 150
key =
key =
key =
shot 01-keyframe-marks
state
